"""{name}: the core of this chip, written in MyHDL.

This starter copies the Tiny Tapeout example: `uo_out` is `ui_in + uio_in`, computed
combinationally. Replace the body with your own design. Keep the parameter names: they are the
Tiny Tapeout pins, and the generated `tt_um_{name}` wrapper connects them by name.

This starter is purely combinational, so it leaves out `clk`, `rst_n` and `ena`. MyHDL treats a
port that nothing reads as an error. When your design needs the clock, add `clk` (and `rst_n`)
to the parameters and use them.

Pin summary (a design may use any subset):
  inputs   clk, rst_n (active low), ena, ui_in[7:0], uio_in[7:0]
  outputs  uo_out[7:0], uio_out[7:0], uio_oe[7:0]
"""

from myhdl import always_comb, block


@block
def {name}(ui_in, uo_out, uio_in, uio_out, uio_oe):
    @always_comb
    def logic():
        uo_out.next = (ui_in + uio_in) % 256
        uio_out.next = 0
        uio_oe.next = 0  # 0 = every bidirectional pin is an input

    return logic
